{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SpTm4FGzmuUrYrdm1AVCPi","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd012-true\n     2→title: \"cmd/true: Exit Successfully\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  The true utility is among the simplest POSIX commands: it does nothing and exits\n     6→  with code 0. Despite its simplicity, a formal specification is needed to ensure the\n     7→  Go implementation matches GNU coreutils true exactly — including --help and --version\n     8→  flag behavior, exit codes for each case, and SIGPIPE handling. The reference binary\n     9→  for differential testing is gtrue (Homebrew GNU coreutils with g- prefix). Without a\n    10→  specification, even a trivial utility can diverge on flag parsing or signal behavior.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Exit with code 0 unconditionally in normal operation (no flags).\n    14→  - G2: Support --help flag that prints usage information to stdout and exits 0.\n    15→  - G3: Support --version flag that prints version string to stdout and exits 0.\n    16→  - G4: Handle SIGPIPE cleanly by exiting without error.\n    17→\n    18→requirements:\n    19→  R1:\n    20→    title: Default behavior (no flags, no arguments)\n    21→    items:\n    22→      - R1.1: Must exit with code 0 when invoked with no arguments.\n    23→      - R1.2: Must produce no output on stdout in normal operation (no flags).\n    24→      - R1.3: Must produce no output on stderr in normal operation (no flags).\n    25→      - R1.4: Must not read from stdin. Stdin must be left untouched.\n    26→      - R1.5: Must ignore any non-flag arguments passed on the command line. Arguments that do not start with \"--\" must not cause an error or change the exit code.\n    27→\n    28→  R2:\n    29→    title: Flag behavior\n    30→    items:\n    31→      - R2.1: --help must print a usage summary to stdout and exit with code 0. The usage text must include the utility name and a brief description of its purpose.\n    32→      - R2.2: --version must print a version string to stdout and exit with code 0. The version string is set at build time via -ldflags; it defaults to \"dev\" when unset.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Signal handling\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: |\n    38→          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream\n    39→          consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c,\n    40→          syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine\n    41→          call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore.\n    42→\n    43→  R4:\n    44→    title: Exit codes\n    45→    items:\n    46→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 in all cases — normal operation, --help, --version, and SIGPIPE.\n    47→      - R4.2: Must exit 0 even when unknown arguments are passed (true never fails).\n    48→\n    49→  R5:\n    50→    title: Differential testing\n    51→    items:\n    52→      - R5.1: The reference binary for differential testing is gtrue (Homebrew GNU coreutils).\n    53→      - R5.2: Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C in the environment.\n    54→\n    55→non_goals:\n    56→  - cmd/true does not implement any I/O operations beyond flag output (--help, --version).\n    57→  - cmd/true does not implement short flags (-h, -v) for help and version; only GNU long flags (--help, --version) are supported, matching gtrue behavior.\n    58→  - cmd/true does not validate or interpret non-flag arguments.\n    59→\n    60→acceptance_criteria:\n    61→  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/true exits with code 0 and produces no stdout or stderr output.\"\n    62→  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/true --help prints usage to stdout and exits 0, matching gtrue --help output structure.\"\n    63→  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/true --version prints a version string to stdout and exits 0.\"\n    64→  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/true with arbitrary non-flag arguments (e.g., true foo bar) exits with code 0.\"\n    65→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gtrue passes for no-args, --help, and --version modes.\"\n    66→  - \"AC6: cmd/true compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    67→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19765b1f-9114-4d67-82a5-32cc28f2b8ed","uuid":"e066a8e6-8e54-41b9-b1c5-d8c34eb19a1a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/257/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd012-true.yaml","content":"id: prd012-true\ntitle: \"cmd/true: Exit Successfully\"\n\nproblem: |\n  The true utility is among the simplest POSIX commands: it does nothing and exits\n  with code 0. Despite its simplicity, a formal specification is needed to ensure the\n  Go implementation matches GNU coreutils true exactly — including --help and --version\n  flag behavior, exit codes for each case, and SIGPIPE handling. The reference binary\n  for differential testing is gtrue (Homebrew GNU coreutils with g- prefix). Without a\n  specification, even a trivial utility can diverge on flag parsing or signal behavior.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Exit with code 0 unconditionally in normal operation (no flags).\n  - G2: Support --help flag that prints usage information to stdout and exits 0.\n  - G3: Support --version flag that prints version string to stdout and exits 0.\n  - G4: Handle SIGPIPE cleanly by exiting without error.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags, no arguments)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must exit with code 0 when invoked with no arguments.\n      - R1.2: Must produce no output on stdout in normal operation (no flags).\n      - R1.3: Must produce no output on stderr in normal operation (no flags).\n      - R1.4: Must not read from stdin. Stdin must be left untouched.\n      - R1.5: Must ignore any non-flag arguments passed on the command line. Arguments that do not start with \"--\" must not cause an error or change the exit code.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag behavior\n    items:\n      - R2.1: --help must print a usage summary to stdout and exit with code 0. The usage text must include the utility name and a brief description of its purpose.\n      - R2.2: --version must print a version string to stdout and exit with code 0. The version string is set at build time via -ldflags; it defaults to \"dev\" when unset.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Signal handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream\n          consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c,\n          syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine\n          call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 in all cases — normal operation, --help, --version, and SIGPIPE.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 0 even when unknown arguments are passed (true never fails).\n\n  R5:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R5.1: The reference binary for differential testing is gtrue (Homebrew GNU coreutils).\n      - R5.2: Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C in the environment.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/true does not implement any I/O operations beyond flag output (--help, --version).\n  - cmd/true does not implement short flags (-h, -v) for help and version; only GNU long flags (--help, --version) are supported, matching gtrue behavior.\n  - cmd/true does not validate or interpret non-flag arguments.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: go run ./cmd/true exits with code 0 and produces no stdout or stderr output.\"\n  - \"AC2: go run ./cmd/true --help prints usage to stdout and exits 0, matching gtrue --help output structure.\"\n  - \"AC3: go run ./cmd/true --version prints a version string to stdout and exits 0.\"\n  - \"AC4: go run ./cmd/true with arbitrary non-flag arguments (e.g., true foo bar) exits with code 0.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gtrue passes for no-args, --help, and --version modes.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/true compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
